<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.13.4: Interpreting Features of an Exponential Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01487</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.13.4: Interpreting Features of an Exponential Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1f8567bc-1636-496a-a6fa-740d4d7a85c6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here are two graphs representing the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> and the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> defined by
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="A graph with two exponential curves, f and g.  Line g has three labeled points: (0, 3), (1, 12), and (2, 48). The points are marked with black dots. The x- and y-axes are shown. Line f has a large y-intercept than line g and intersects with line g.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_09_01.jpg" width="300"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">In function g, is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> greater than or less than 2? Explain how you know.</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00006"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is greater than 2.</para>
            <list id="list-00001">
              <item>The graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> starts off being below the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, but later it surpasses the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Since
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> grows more quickly than <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the growth factor <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> must be greater than the growth factor for
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, which is 2.</item>
              <item>The growth factor from the first point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> to the second point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is 4 because
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and 4 is greater than 2.</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Write an equation that defines <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Explain your answer.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00010"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> because the initial value is 3 and the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-value is multiplied by 4 when the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-value
    increases by 1, so the growth factor is 4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number, in thousands, of social media followers of two organizations as a
        function of years since 2010. What does the intersection of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> mean in this context?</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00014">It represents the time, in years since 2010, when the two organizations have the same number of followers.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content></document>
